+### Bayes Factor
+A rank-based statistical measure quantifying evidence across views for criteria with per-view decomposition (similarity, Pearson, Spearman), supplementing binary pass/fail decisions [S3, unofficial]. It pools all $2n$ views similarity scores (reconstruction and baseline) and ranks them, then computes BF+0 from the resulting two-sample t-statistic under a Jeffreys-Zellner-Siow prior (Cauchy scale $r = \sqrt{2}/2$), testing the directional hypothesis $H^+$: mean rank of the reconstruction exceeds that of the baseline [S3, unofficial]. It is not applicable to RSA because RSA produces a single scalar per epoch with strong autocorrelation and no semantically grounded pairing between epochs, violating the exchangeability assumption required for the rank-based test [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of using probability distributions for volume rendering classification originated with Drebin et al. in 1988, who proposed using Bayesian estimation to segment CT data based on known X-ray absorption probabilities of materials like air, fat, soft tissue, and bone [S2, unofficial]. This approach moved away from hard thresholds to handle the ambiguity of partial volume effects [G]. Later work expanded this to include statistical metrics (mean, variance) to capture local texture [9488-statistical-metrics-based-transfer-function.md] and Gaussian Mixture Models for uncertainty visualization [5862-local-data-models-for-probabilistic-transfer-function-design.md]. More recently, machine learning techniques like SVMs [6287-support-vector-machine-svm-classification.md] and differentiable rendering frameworks [11686-distribution-families-for-differentiable-rendering.md] have been integrated into this family to automate and optimize transfer function design.
